# ~pgaupload/.ssh/authorized_keys on the staging server (developer.pgadmin.org)
#
# Two keys, two jobs, two blast radii. Neither of them can give you a shell.
#
# Placeholders in angle brackets must be substituted at install time:
#
#   <...KEY...>   the public key, one line, no line breaks
#
# Options used, and why:
#
#   restrict      Refuse everything optional, then add back nothing. This is
#                 the modern shorthand for no-agent-forwarding, no-pty,
#                 no-port-forwarding, no-X11-forwarding and no-user-rc, and
#                 crucially it is future-proof: when OpenSSH grows a new
#                 forwarding feature, `restrict` disables that too, whereas an
#                 explicit list of no-* options silently would not.
#
#                 no-pty matters more than it looks. Without it, a client can
#                 ask for a terminal, and a terminal is an interactive channel
#                 into whatever the forced command turns out to be.
#
#                 There is deliberately no from= here. Restricting a key to
#                 the addresses it should be used from would be worth having,
#                 but these files are generated rather than hand written and
#                 that option is not among the ones we can set, so the whole
#                 of the containment rests on restrict and command= instead.
#                 That is the part which decides what a key can do; what a
#                 from= would have added is a second answer to where it can be
#                 used from, which is a loss worth knowing about rather than a
#                 hole: a stolen key still gets one verb, or one rsync
#                 confined to one directory, and no shell either way.
#
#   command=      The forced command. Whatever the client asked to run is put
#                 in SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND and this runs instead. The wrapper
#                 parses that variable itself and never lets a shell near it.
#
# There is deliberately no environment= option on either key. The wrapper
# builds its children's environment from scratch, but not offering the client
# an input channel at all is better than filtering one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key 1: publishing. Verbs only. No file content passes through this key.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a holder of this key can do here: create a staging directory from
# a fixed template, list staging directories, and rebuild and sign the apt and
# yum metadata inside one. That is the entire capability set; it is enumerated
# in the VERBS table in pga-publish, on one screen, on purpose.
restrict,command="/usr/local/sbin/pga-publish" ssh-ed25519 <...PUBLISH-KEY...> pga-publish@runner

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key 2: upload. Bytes only. No verbs, no metadata, no signing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# rrsync ships with rsync (on Debian and Ubuntu, /usr/share/doc/rsync/scripts,
# or /usr/bin/rrsync on newer packages: check before writing this line). It is
# itself a forced-command wrapper, written and reviewed by the rsync
# maintainers, whose whole job is to inspect the rsync argument list the client
# sent and refuse anything outside the subtree it was given.
#
#   -wo        write-only. The client may send files and may not fetch them.
#              A compromised runner therefore cannot read back whatever else
#              lives under the staging root, and cannot use this key to
#              exfiltrate anything.
#   -no-del    no deletion. Uploads add and overwrite; they never remove. A
#              wrong --delete in a workflow, or a deliberate one from an
#              attacker, stops here rather than emptying developer.pgadmin.org.
#   -munge     munge incoming symlinks so that they cannot be followed. Without
#              it, an upload can drop a symlink pointing at, say, the account's
#              ~/.ssh, and the next thing that writes through that path writes
#              somewhere it should not.
#
# The trailing path is the root the client is confined to. Every path the
# client names is resolved relative to it, and rrsync refuses anything that
# climbs out, so the client never sees an absolute path at all.
restrict,command="/usr/bin/rrsync -wo -no-del -munge /var/www/html/builds" ssh-ed25519 <...UPLOAD-KEY...> pga-upload@runner

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key 3: the download server's read-only pull.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The download server fetches staging content from here during publication, so
# it holds a
# key here. It is read-only and confined to the same staging root, which is a
# tree that developer.pgadmin.org already serves over plain HTTP; this edge
# therefore grants that server nothing it could not have fetched with curl,
# and it
# is worth saying so explicitly in review, because "the download server can SSH
# to the staging server" sounds worse than it is.
#
# If that reasoning is ever rejected, the alternative is for the runner to pull
# the
# content down and push it up again over its own two keys, at the cost of
# moving every byte twice. The wrapper would lose release-fetch,
# packages-fetch and snapshot-fetch, and gain nothing else.
restrict,command="/usr/bin/rrsync -ro /var/www/html/builds" ssh-ed25519 <...PULL-KEY...> pga-pull@download
